    "description": "The Claims mobile application and ClaimsProvider service are built and released by separate teams. The mobile client relies on specific interactions that are not obvious from the service documentation: it sends a GET /claims/{id} request with an X-Tenant-ID header, expects a particular status when a claim has no settlement, and depends on the exact field names and value types in the response. The provider team wants to replace its database access layer without requiring a shared staging system or coordinating releases with the mobile team. The mobile team's existing request-level tests already exercise these expectations. The provider must run those concrete interaction checks inside its own build before merging an internal change, and the mobile release pipeline must not need access to a live provider deployment. A shared end-to-end environment is unavailable, while the provider can expose the relevant endpoint in a local verification process.",
    "decision_statement": "Decide how the ClaimsProvider build will verify the concrete API interactions relied upon by the independently released Claims client.",
    "notes": "The provider build runs the mobile client's concrete interaction checks locally instead of sharing a staging environment (consumer-driven-contract-test); characterization-test is the near-miss as the family's only sibling."
